// The AI orchestration layer: the provider-agnostic tool-calling loop. The single seam between the
// loop and a concrete model API is ONE request — `infer_step` — so a provider is a module that serves
// it (`use gemini.provider(...)` / `use openai.provider(...)` at the app root) and this module never
// names a provider. Adding a provider is adding a module; nothing here changes.
//
// Everything else is plain Katari over the agnostic vocabulary in `ai.types`: derive each
// tool's schema with `reflection.get_metadata`, ask the ambient provider for a step, dispatch tool
// calls concurrently with `parallel for` (each validated against its tool's schema by
// `reflection.call_agent` — an `as_tool` tool from an MCP server validates against the server's
// schema the same way), and feed the results back until the model answers.
//
// Files flow through the loop as blob handles: a tool result's `file` values are collected off the
// result's wire form (`collect_result_files`) and ride the `tool_result`, so the provider can inline
// their content for the model while the result is fresh — and the model can replay any handle it has
// seen into a later tool call (`view_file` re-reads one on demand). WHICH content a provider can inline
// is one decision over the file's recorded content type — the `file_kind` sum below, which every
// provider dispatches over.

import ai.types

@"One model step over the conversation + tool schemas — THE provider seam. A provider module serves
this request for the extent of a continuation (`use gemini.provider(...)`), so the loop stays
provider-agnostic and providers stay loop-agnostic. The reply is an OUTCOME sum, never a throw: on
success `inferred(result)` carries the step's decision AND its token accounting (`types.step_result`,
so any loop over this seam can meter its context for free); on failure `inference_failed(error)`
carries the `step_error` the provider gave up on (its retries exhausted, or a fatal status). Handing the
failure back as a VALUE — rather than throwing from the provider's DEEP handler — is what lets each loop
decide at its own PERFORM site whether to absorb the failure (the region loop abandons just that turn and
lives on) or re-raise it into its own caller (the one-shot loops): a deep handler can only throw OUT of
its own context, never INTO the loop's, so the resume value is the only channel that reaches the loop."
request infer_step(history: array[types.message], tool_metas: array[reflection.agent_metadata]) -> step_outcome

// The error surface of one model step: malformed provider JSON (`json.parse_error`), a non-2xx API
// status (`http.status_error`), or a transport failure (`http.fetch_error`). A provider no longer THROWS
// this — it hands it back as `inference_failed(error)` (see `infer_step`), so the failure surfaces at the
// loop's perform site, not the provider's deep use site. A provider builds its request body as a raw value
// tree and posts it with `http.post_json`, which parses the reply — so the only JSON failure a step can
// carry is that parse. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type step_error = json.parse_error | http.status_error | http.fetch_error | empty_reply

@"One model step's OUTCOME success: the provider produced a step (`types.step_result` for `infer_step`,
`types.object_result` for `infer_object`). Generic over the payload so both seam requests share one sum shape."
data inferred[T](@"The step the provider produced." result: T)

@"One model step's OUTCOME failure: the provider gave up — a transient error past its retry budget, or a
fatal status / malformed body. Carried back as a VALUE so a loop handles it at its perform site, rather than
the provider throwing out of its own deep handler and unwinding the whole loop with it."
data inference_failed(@"The step error the provider surfaced." error: step_error)

// The outcome of one `infer_step`: the produced step, or the provider's surfaced failure. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type step_outcome = inferred[types.step_result] | inference_failed

// The outcome of one `infer_object`: the produced object, or the provider's surfaced failure. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type object_outcome = inferred[types.object_result] | inference_failed

@"One structured-output step over the conversation — the seam's second request, served by the same
provider handler as `infer_step`. @schema@ is the JSON Schema the reply must conform to (each
provider maps it onto its native constrained decoding); the reply is an OUTCOME — `inferred(result)`
with the decoded object and usage, or `inference_failed(error)`. Prefer `infer_structured[T]`, which
reifies T's schema, validates the reply, and re-raises a failure at your own call site."
request infer_object(history: array[types.message], schema: unknown) -> object_outcome

@"Infer a single reply with no tools (the common chat case). A provider failure re-raises here as
`prelude.throw[step_error]` — this is a one-shot call, so a caller that wants to survive a failed step
installs its own handler (the region loop does not use this; it drives the seam directly and absorbs)."
agent reply(history: array[types.message]) -> string {
  match (infer_step(history = history, tool_metas = [])) {
    case inferred(result => result) -> {
      match (result.step) {
        case types.step_final(text => text) -> text
        case types.step_call(calls => _) -> "(unexpected tool call without tools)"
      }
    }
    case inference_failed(error => error) -> prelude.throw(error = error)
  }
}

@"Infer a single reply as a typed value — structured output with the type as the contract:
`reflection.schema_of[T]` reifies T's schema, the ambient provider decodes against it natively
(Gemini's responseSchema, OpenAI's response_format, Anthropic a forced tool), and the decoded object
validates as T (`json.validate` — a nonconforming reply throws `json.validation_error`). Instantiate
T explicitly; it appears only in the result, so it is never inferable. An object type
(`type shape = { ... }`) makes the friendliest model-facing schema — a data type asks the model to
produce its `$katari_constructor` wrapper too."
agent infer_structured[T](history: array[types.message]) -> T {
  match (infer_object(history = history, schema = reflection.schema_of[T]())) {
    case inferred(result => result) -> json.validate[T](value = result.value)
    case inference_failed(error => error) -> prelude.throw(error = error)
  }
}

// ── transient retry: the provider layer's resilience ──────────────────────────
//
// A provider's `infer_step` / `infer_object` handler runs at the app root (its `use` site), ABOVE the
// loop — so a step failure (`step_error`) surfaces THERE and the tool loop cannot catch it (the loop is the
// handler's continuation, not an enclosing handler). A transient failure — a 429 rate limit, a 5xx, a
// dropped connection — would therefore end a long-lived bot. The fix lives where the error IS catchable:
// inside the provider's own handler body, whose effect row is CONCRETE, so a `prelude.throw[step_error]`
// handler discharges it (the same shape as an app's resilient `try_send`). Each provider retries a
// transient step with exponential backoff; these two pure helpers make the decision, and the retry loop
// itself lives in each handler body — a callback-wrapped `retry(task)` would commonise the whole thing,
// which is not the point (the decision is the shared part, not the control flow).

@"The provider's API finished a step WITHOUT an answer: no tool call and no reply text, with a
finish reason other than a normal stop (an output-token truncation that ate the whole answer, a
safety block). Deliberately distinct from a NORMAL empty reply (finish reason STOP), which stays a
legitimate quiet `step_final(\"\")` — the region loop's monitors depend on it. Not transient: the
same context replays into the same truncation, so it surfaces to the app instead of burning retries."
data empty_reply(@"The provider-reported finish reason (e.g. \"MAX_TOKENS\")." finish_reason: string)

@"Whether a step error is TRANSIENT — worth a backoff and a retry — as opposed to fatal. A transport
failure (`http.fetch_error`: DNS, a refused connection, a timeout, a mid-flight restart) is always
transient; a status error is transient for 408 (request timeout), 429 (rate limit) and any 5xx
(server-side), and fatal for every other status (401/403/404 … — a retry will not fix them); a
`json.parse_error` is fatal (a malformed body will not parse on a retry)."
agent is_transient(error: step_error) -> boolean {
  match (error) {
    case http.fetch_error(message => _) -> true
    case http.status_error(status => status, body => _) -> status == 408 || status == 429 || status >= 500
    case json.parse_error(message => _) -> false
    case empty_reply(finish_reason => _) -> false
  }
}

@"The backoff delay in milliseconds before retry @attempt@ (0-based): @base_ms@ doubled per attempt (base,
2×base, 4×base, …) and capped at one minute, so a long outage does not sleep unboundedly between the final
tries."
agent backoff_ms(attempt: integer, base_ms: number) -> number {
  let grown = for (let _step in array.range(start = 0, end = attempt), var delay: number = base_ms) {
    next with { delay = delay * 2.0 }
  } then (_steps) { delay }
  math.min(left = grown, right = 60000.0)
}

@"The outcome of ONE provider step attempt, threaded back to a provider's retry loop: the step's @result@
on success. A provider's per-attempt helper catches a `step_error` into `attempt_err` so the loop can
classify it (`is_transient`) and either back off and retry or propagate."
data attempt_ok[T](result: T)

@"The outcome of ONE provider step attempt: the @error@ the attempt caught, for the retry loop to classify."
data attempt_err(error: step_error)

// A step attempt's verdict: the result, or the caught error to classify and maybe retry. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type attempt[T] = attempt_ok[T] | attempt_err

@"Two tools in one loop's tool list share a metadata name, so the model's pick could not be routed
unambiguously (dispatch matches by name — the first match would silently win, and the other tool
would be unreachable). A configuration defect the app fixes — a `prefix` on one of the colliding MCP
connections, or a rename — typed so a supervisor reports it instead of the run panicking."
data duplicate_tool(@"The name two tools share." name: string)

@"Validate one step's assembled tool metadata: every name unique, or the typed `duplicate_tool`.
Every loop runs its tool list through this once per turn, so a colliding list fails loudly at the
first step instead of silently routing every pick to whichever tool sorts first."
agent ensure_unique_names(metas: array[reflection.agent_metadata]) -> array[reflection.agent_metadata] {
  for (let meta in metas, var seen: record[boolean] = record.empty()) {
    if (record.has(target = seen, key = meta.name)) {
      prelude.throw(error = duplicate_tool(name = meta.name))
    } else {
      next with { seen = record.set(target = seen, key = meta.name, value = true) }
    }
  } then (_metas) { null }
  metas
}

// ── attached files: one dispatch over the recorded content type ───────────────
//
// How a file reaches a model follows from ONE datum: its recorded content type. `classify_file` turns
// that string into the closed `file_kind` sum, and each provider MATCHES the sum — one arm per wire
// shape its own API accepts (Anthropic's `document` block, Gemini's `inlineData` part, a text block, or
// the note that says plainly the content cannot be read). The classification lives here, once, so the
// three providers cannot drift on what counts as textual; the arms live in the providers, because only
// a provider knows its own wire.
//
// The content type IS the data and it is taken as recorded: nothing here reads a file's bytes to guess a
// better type. A sender that labels a .md as `application/octet-stream` therefore lands in
// `opaque_file`, and the model is told it cannot read it — the fix belongs at the upload boundary that
// recorded the type, not in a sniffer here that would sometimes be wrong about a file the model then
// answers from.

@"An image the model can look at (`image/*`). @content_type@ is the exact recorded type because that is
what the wire's `media_type` / `mimeType` field must carry — the family decided the arm, the exact type
rides along as its datum."
data image_file(@"The file's recorded content type." content_type: string)

@"A PDF (`application/pdf`): a document a model reads as pages — text, tables and figures alike — on a
provider whose API has a document shape for it (Anthropic's `document` block, Gemini's `inlineData`)."
data document_file(@"The file's recorded content type." content_type: string)

@"A file whose bytes ARE text: any `text/*` (plain, markdown, csv, html, …), plus `application/json`,
`application/xml` and `application/csv`. This arm needs no API support at all — the decoded text inlines
as ordinary text (capped; see `file_text_note`), so every provider serves it."
data text_file(@"The file's recorded content type." content_type: string)

@"Audio (`audio/*`): inlinable on a provider whose model takes audio input, a note to the model on one
that does not."
data audio_file(@"The file's recorded content type." content_type: string)

@"Video (`video/*`): inlinable on a provider whose model takes video input, a note to the model on one
that does not."
data video_file(@"The file's recorded content type." content_type: string)

@"A file no arm above covers — an archive, an office document, a font, an unrecorded or gone handle.
No provider can put its content in front of the model, so every provider reports it as unreadable
instead of shipping a bare handle the model would answer from."
data opaque_file(@"The file's recorded content type; \"\" when none was recorded." content_type: string)

// How ONE attached file can reach a model, off its recorded content type. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type file_kind = image_file | document_file | text_file | audio_file | video_file | opaque_file

@"The file's recorded content type, or \"\" when the handle is gone (freed, or an id that never named a
live blob). A gone file has no content to inline — the model is told so — so `gone` folds to the same
unreadable signal as an unrecorded type and never fails a whole step over one stale attachment."
agent file_content_type(@"The file to read." attachment: file) -> string {
  // `files.content_type` throws `files.gone` for a stale handle. Catch exactly that and recover with ""
  // (an unrecorded type, hence `opaque_file`), so one stale attachment never fails a whole step.
  use handler {
    request prelude.throw(error: files.gone) -> never { break "" }
  }
  files.content_type(value = attachment)
}

@"@content_type@ in the one form both a dispatch and a wire field can use: the media type alone,
lowercased, with any parameter dropped (\"Text/Plain; charset=utf-8\" becomes \"text/plain\"). RFC 2045
makes a media type case-insensitive and lets a sender append parameters, and an HTTP download records
exactly those forms — so the raw string is the wrong thing to compare (`application/pdf; version=1.7`
would miss the PDF arm and read as unreadable) and the wrong thing to ship (a provider's `media_type`
field takes the bare type)."
agent normalized_content_type(@"The recorded content type." content_type: string) -> string {
  let without_parameters = match (string.index_of(value = content_type, search = ";")) {
    case null -> content_type
    case index -> string.slice(value = content_type, start = 0, end = index)
  }
  string.to_lower(value = string.trim(value = without_parameters))
}

@"The content type's FAMILY — the token before the \"/\" (\"image\" for \"image/png\"), or the whole
string when there is no slash. The family is the dispatch key: every `image/*` reaches a model the same
way, so a provider needs one arm per family rather than one per media type."
agent content_type_family(@"The recorded content type." content_type: string) -> string {
  match (string.index_of(value = content_type, search = "/")) {
    case null -> content_type
    case index -> string.slice(value = content_type, start = 0, end = index)
  }
}

@"How @attachment@ can reach a model, from its recorded content type alone — the `file_kind` sum every
provider dispatches over. The `application/*` types that are not their own family are named exactly (a
PDF is a document; JSON / XML / CSV are text), and everything else falls to its family. The type the
verdict carries is the NORMALIZED one, so a provider ships a `media_type` its API accepts whatever form
the sender recorded."
agent classify_file(@"The file to classify." attachment: file) -> file_kind {
  let content_type = normalized_content_type(content_type = file_content_type(attachment = attachment))
  match (content_type) {
    case "application/pdf" -> document_file(content_type = content_type)
    case "application/json" -> text_file(content_type = content_type)
    case "application/xml" -> text_file(content_type = content_type)
    case "application/csv" -> text_file(content_type = content_type)
    case _ -> classify_content_type_family(content_type = content_type)
  }
}

@"The family half of `classify_file`: `image/*`, `text/*`, `audio/*`, `video/*` — and `opaque_file` for
any other family, an unrecorded type included."
agent classify_content_type_family(@"The recorded content type." content_type: string) -> file_kind {
  match (content_type_family(content_type = content_type)) {
    case "image" -> image_file(content_type = content_type)
    case "text" -> text_file(content_type = content_type)
    case "audio" -> audio_file(content_type = content_type)
    case "video" -> video_file(content_type = content_type)
    case _ -> opaque_file(content_type = content_type)
  }
}

// The notes below name no OTHER package's tool, on purpose. This package cannot know which tools the app
// handed the model, and a note promising one the holder lacks sends the model chasing a tool that is not
// there — which is exactly how `send_files` stayed advertised here long after the chat packages deleted
// it. State what the handle IS and let the model match it against the tool list it actually holds;
// `view_file` is the one tool this package owns, so it is the one a note may name.

@"A file as its `$katari_ref` handle note — the resting representation every provider shows for an
attachment. The handle is the exact object a model replays into a tool call, so the note IS the
affordance and not a description: any tool the model holds that takes a file accepts this value."
agent file_handle_note(@"The file to note." attachment: file) -> string {
  f"[attached file: ${json.stringify(value = attachment)}]"
}

@"How a content type reads inside a note: the type itself, or the statement that none was recorded (a
bare \"\" tells a model nothing)."
agent content_type_label(@"The recorded content type." content_type: string) -> string {
  match (content_type) {
    case "" -> "no recorded content type"
    case recorded -> f"content type ${recorded}"
  }
}

@"The note for a file this model connection cannot show: the handle, the content type, and the plain
statement that the CONTENT is absent. Stating the limit is the whole point — handed a bare handle with
no content and no explanation, a model answers from the file's look and invents the rest, so the note
tells it to report the gap instead."
agent unreadable_file_note(
  @"The file to note." attachment: file,
  @"Its recorded content type." content_type: string,
) -> string {
  f"[attached file: ${json.stringify(value = attachment)} — ${content_type_label(content_type = content_type)}. Its CONTENT is not available to you: this model connection cannot read that content type. Say so if you are asked about it; do not guess what it contains.]"
}

@"A textual file as text the model reads: a header naming the handle and the content type, then the
decoded content, cut at @max_characters@ code points with an explicit marker when it overflows. The
marker is not decoration — a model that cannot tell it is holding a FRAGMENT answers confidently from
the fragment. The 24000-code-point default is ~6k tokens: half again the `web` package's page budget,
because an attachment cannot be re-read at another offset the way a page can be re-fetched, and still
small enough that several attachments fit one turn."
agent file_text_note(
  @"The file to inline." attachment: file,
  @"Its recorded content type." content_type: string,
  @"The truncation budget, in code points." max_characters: integer ?= 24000,
) -> string {
  let text = file_text(attachment = attachment)
  let length = string.length(value = text)
  let header = f"[attached file: ${json.stringify(value = attachment)} — ${content_type_label(content_type = content_type)}, ${length} characters. Its text follows.]"
  if (length > max_characters) {
    let head = string.slice(value = text, start = 0, end = max_characters)
    f"${header}\n${head}\n[… TRUNCATED: the first ${max_characters} of ${length} characters are shown; the rest was not sent. Treat this as a fragment, and say so rather than answering as if you had the whole file.]"
  } else {
    f"${header}\n${text}"
  }
}

@"A file's bytes decoded as UTF-8 text — the read behind `file_text_note`. The whole content transits the
value plane because the runtime has no partial-read primitive; it is a PRIM read, recomputed on replay
rather than recorded, so nothing durable grows by the file's size. A gone handle reads as \"\", the same
recovery as `file_content_type` (classification already routes a gone handle to `opaque_file`, so this is
totality for the vanishing case of a file freed between the two reads)."
agent file_text(@"The file to read." attachment: file) -> string {
  use handler {
    request prelude.throw(error: files.gone) -> never { break "" }
  }
  string.from_base64(value = files.read_base64(value = attachment))
}

// ── the inline window: how far back a file's CONTENT is re-sent ────────────────
//
// Every step re-sends the whole conversation, so a file that inlines forever is uploaded again on every
// step: a 10 MB PDF in a thirty-step tool loop is 300 MB of request bodies and thirty upload waits, which a
// human watching a chat channel feels as latency. Prompt caching does not help — it saves the model's
// COMPUTE on a prefix it has seen, not the client's bytes on the wire.
//
// So only the newest @keep@ FILE-BEARING history elements inline their content; older files show their
// handle note alone, and the model re-reads any of them on demand with `view_file`. That recovery path is
// what makes the degradation honest, and it now exists on all three providers (a tool result's files reach
// the model everywhere), which is what it took for this policy to be safe.
//
// KEYED TO FILES, NOT TO POSITION, and that is the whole design. A positional window ("the last element
// inlines") slides on EVERY step: each tool call appends an element, the element that inlined a moment ago
// degrades to a note, and the rendered prefix changes underneath a conversation that did not gain a single
// file — which invalidates the prompt-cache prefix on every step of a tool loop, exactly the thing the
// caching breakpoints were placed to protect. Keyed to files, the window moves only when a NEW file
// arrives; an element without files renders identically inlined or not, so appending tool calls, tool
// results and model turns leaves every earlier byte alone. A new attachment does rewrite the tail, at a
// turn boundary the app was paying for anyway.

@"How many files a history element carries — a turn's attachments, or every file across a result batch.
The count is what makes an element FILE-BEARING; a call turn never carries one."
agent message_file_count(@"The history element to weigh." message: types.message) -> integer {
  match (message) {
    case types.turn(role => _, text => _, files => files, author => _) -> array.length(target = files)
    case types.call_turn(calls => _) -> 0
    case types.result_turn(results => results) -> {
      for (let result in results, var total: integer = 0) {
        next with { total = total + array.length(target = result.files) }
      } then (_results) { total }
    }
  }
}

@"The index of the OLDEST history element whose files still inline: the newest @keep@ FILE-BEARING elements
inline their content, and everything before this index shows handle notes only. A history with no file at
all answers its own length — there is nothing to inline, and the split is empty either way.
Providers render `slice(0, index)` with inlining off and `slice(index, length)` with it on, so the whole
policy is one integer and each provider keeps its own rendering."
agent inline_from_index(
  @"The conversation so far." history: array[types.message],
  @"How many of the newest file-bearing elements keep their content inline." keep: integer ?= 1,
) -> integer {
  let bearing = for (let message in history, var count: integer = 0) {
    if (message_file_count(message = message) == 0) {
      next with { count = count }
    } else {
      next with { count = count + 1 }
    }
  } then (_messages) { count }
  // One flag per element: an element inlines when it carries files AND is among the newest @keep@ of the
  // file-bearing ones (`seen` counts those before it, so this one is the `seen + 1`-th).
  let flags = for (let message in history, var seen: integer = 0) {
    if (message_file_count(message = message) == 0) {
      next false with { seen = seen }
    } else {
      next bearing - seen <= keep with { seen = seen + 1 }
    }
  } then (marks) { marks }
  // The FIRST inlining element is the boundary; the elements after it that carry no file render the same
  // either way, so one index describes the window exactly.
  match (array.index_of(target = flags, value = true)) {
    case null -> array.length(target = history)
    case index -> index
  }
}

@"Tool: read a file that is in this conversation — an image, a PDF, or a text document. Pass the file's
handle object; a bare {\"$katari_ref\": \"<id>\"} suffices (copy the id from the conversation).
A text document (`text/*`, JSON, XML, CSV) comes back AS TEXT in this tool's result, cut at a cap with an
explicit truncation marker when it is long. An image, a PDF, audio or video comes back as the file itself,
which the model connection places in your context if its API accepts that type — so if the content does
not appear, say you cannot see it rather than guessing. A content type nothing can read (an archive, an
office document, an unrecorded type) comes back as a note saying exactly that, and reporting it is then
the correct answer."
agent view_file(@"The file to read: its handle object, as it appears in the conversation." attachment: file) -> file | string {
  // The same content-type dispatch the providers run, at the one point where an arm can answer with a VALUE
  // instead of a wire block — which is why two arms answer here rather than deferring: text needs no API
  // support, so it reads identically on every provider, and an unreadable type is unreadable on all of them,
  // so saying so beats handing back a bare handle the model would answer from. Every other kind returns the
  // file for the provider to inline through whichever block its own API accepts.
  match (classify_file(attachment = attachment)) {
    case text_file(content_type => content_type) -> file_text_note(attachment = attachment, content_type = content_type)
    case opaque_file(content_type => content_type) -> unreadable_file_note(attachment = attachment, content_type = content_type)
    case _ -> attachment
  }
}

@"Run the tool-using model loop, generic over the tools' shared effect @E@: derive each tool's schema
with `reflection.get_metadata`, then up to @max_steps@ times perform `infer_step` against the ambient
provider; on a batch of tool calls, dispatch them all CONCURRENTLY with `parallel for` (each resolves
its tool by name and invokes it by value via `reflection.call_agent`, validating the model's args
against the tool's schema), then feed every result back as a new turn; on a final answer, return it."
agent infer_with_tools[effect E lacks time.race_settled](
  history: array[types.message],
  tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  max_steps: integer,
  @"The per-tool-call budget in milliseconds, or `null` (the default) for no deadline — see `take_turn`." tool_budget_milliseconds: number | null ?= null,
  @"Tool names exempt from the deadline race (human-latency tools) — see `take_turn`." unbounded_tool_names: array[string] ?= [],
) -> string {
  // The one turn loop lives in `take_turn`; this one-shot shape re-raises a failure at the perform
  // site (a one-shot caller has no loop to absorb into) and keeps only the reply.
  match (take_turn[E](conversation = history, tools = tools, max_steps = max_steps, tool_budget_milliseconds = tool_budget_milliseconds, unbounded_tool_names = unbounded_tool_names)) {
    case session_turn(reply => reply_text, conversation => _, used_tokens => _) -> reply_text
    case failed_turn(conversation => _, error => error) -> prelude.throw(error = error)
  }
}

@"WHO a step is on behalf of: the @author@ of the NEWEST user turn in @history@ — taken AS-IS, null
included, with no further scanning back. The newest user turn is the turn that triggered the current
model turn, so a system-triggered turn (a monitor tick, whose injection carries `author = null`)
shields the earlier human turns instead of misattributing the step to whoever spoke last; the
loop-synthesized user turns (the budget close, the empty-final renudge) are also null and likewise
read as unattributed. Providers lift a non-null result into their end-user metadata field
(Anthropic's `metadata.user_id`, OpenAI's `safety_identifier`); Gemini has no such field and ignores
it."
agent attribution(history: array[types.message]) -> string | null {
  for (let message in history, var newest: string | null = null) {
    match (message) {
      case types.turn(role => role, text => _, files => _, author => author) -> {
        // A LATER user turn always overwrites — a null author on the newest user turn deliberately
        // erases an earlier human's tag rather than letting the scan reach past the trigger.
        if (role == "user") { next with { newest = author } } else { next with { newest = newest } }
      }
      case _ -> { next with { newest = newest } }
    }
  } then (_messages) { newest }
}

@"The one-shot renudge a loop plays when the model's final step came back EMPTY: an empty string is
a lost answer in an interactive exchange (a thinking model can finish a heavy turn with only thought
parts and no text), so the loop asks ONCE for a plain-text answer before accepting the silence. The
wording also names the legitimate way out — a watcher protocol with truly nothing to report answers
empty again, and the second empty is accepted as-is."
agent empty_reply_nudge() -> types.message {
  types.turn(
    role = "user",
    text = "(Your previous reply was empty. If you have an answer, state it now in plain text. If you truly have nothing to say or report, reply with an empty message once more.)",
    files = [],
  )
}

@"The forced tool-less close every loop plays when its step budget runs out: a user turn telling the
model the budget is spent, so ONE last step commits to an answer from what the tools already gathered
instead of discarding it."
agent budget_exhausted_turn() -> types.message {
  types.turn(
    role = "user",
    text = "You have used your entire tool-call budget. Do not call any more tools. Answer the original question now, as best you can, using only what you have already gathered.",
    files = [],
  )
}

@"Find the tool whose metadata name is the model's pick and invoke it by value via
`reflection.call_agent` (the model's args ride as a plain value, and the delegation boundary
validates them against the tool's schema — the compiled signature of a Katari agent, or the
server-declared schema of an MCP tool — while the boundary's unconditional wire→value conversion turns
any replayed `$katari_ref` into the `file` it denotes). Every way a call can go wrong is fed back to the
model as an error result it reads and corrects on its next step: a hallucinated name (nothing matches), a
tool that panics, a tool's own typed `throw`, malformed args (a `reflection.call_error` from the
boundary), and a call that outruns @budget_milliseconds@ (raced via `time.with_deadline`, the late
call cancelled) alike — none of them reaches the app root. The result's `file` values are collected off the
result tree so the provider can show them to the model (their `$katari_ref` handles are already visible in
the result text)."
agent dispatch_one[effect E lacks time.race_settled](tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}], call: types.tool_call, @"The per-call budget in milliseconds, or `null` for no deadline." budget_milliseconds: number | null, @"Metadata names exempt from the deadline race — human-latency tools; see `take_turn`." unbounded_tool_names: array[string] ?= []) -> types.tool_result {
  // A failing tool must never fail the conversation — that is the loop's whole premise, and it holds for
  // BOTH ways a call can crash: a `panic` (a crashed handler) and any typed `throw` — the tool's own
  // `throw[my_error]` and the boundary's `throw[call_error]` for hallucinated args alike — fold back HERE
  // as an error result the model reads and corrects on its next step, instead of escalating to the app
  // root. This is why the tools' effect is spelled `{...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}`: `E` stays throw-free,
  // every error a tool can raise rides the EXPLICIT `prelude.throw[unknown]`, and the `throw(error: unknown)`
  // handler below discharges exactly that row — so `E` escalates untouched while every tool failure returns
  // to the model as feedback it can act on (a bad-arguments hallucination becomes self-correcting too).
  use handler {
    request panic(msg: string) {
      break types.tool_result(
        name = call.name,
        call_id = call.opaque,
        text = f"(tool failed: ${msg})",
        files = [],
      )
    }
  }
  use handler {
    request prelude.throw(error: unknown) -> never {
      break types.tool_result(
        name = call.name,
        call_id = call.opaque,
        text = f"(tool failed: ${json.stringify(value = error)})",
        files = [],
      )
    }
  }
  // The result a completed call folds to — shared by the bare and the deadlined path. `stringify`
  // renders the result's wire form (files show as `$katari_ref` handles) for the model, while the
  // actual `file` values are collected off the result tree for inlining.
  agent delivered(result: unknown) -> types.tool_result {
    types.tool_result(
      name = call.name,
      call_id = call.opaque,
      text = json.stringify(value = result),
      files = collect_result_files(node = result),
    )
  }
  for (let tool in tools) {
    let meta = reflection.get_metadata(value = tool)
    if (meta.name == call.name) {
      // The args are already a plain value; the boundary unconditionally turns any replayed `$katari_ref`
      // into a `file`. A `null` budget calls the tool bare; a number races it against the deadline
      // (`time.with_deadline` — first wins, the loser cancelled), so no single tool can hang the turn:
      // the timeout comes back to the model as an error result it reads, like every other tool failure.
      // A name in @unbounded_tool_names@ is exempted the same way a null budget is: deadlines bound
      // MACHINE latency, and a tool legitimately waiting on a human is visible in the admin surface
      // while it waits, so it is left unbounded instead of cancelled mid-ask.
      let effective_budget = if (array.contains(target = unbounded_tool_names, value = call.name)) {
        null
      } else {
        budget_milliseconds
      }
      match (effective_budget) {
        case null -> {
          return delivered(result = reflection.call_agent(target = tool, args = call.args))
        }
        case limit -> {
          agent attempt(value: null) -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]} {
            reflection.call_agent(target = tool, args = call.args)
          }
          match (time.with_deadline(milliseconds = limit, task = attempt)) {
            case time.completed(value => result) -> { return delivered(result = result) }
            case time.deadline_passed() -> {
              return types.tool_result(
                name = call.name,
                call_id = call.opaque,
                text = f"(tool timed out after ${limit}ms; the call was cancelled)",
                files = [],
              )
            }
          }
        }
      }
    } else {
      null
    }
  }
  types.tool_result(
    name = call.name,
    call_id = call.opaque,
    text = f"(error: unknown tool \"${call.name}\")",
    files = [],
  )
}

@"Collect every `file` value out of a tool result's value tree: `reflection.call_agent` returns the
tool's result as a plain value, so a `file` the tool produced (a generated image, an MCP tool's
screenshot) is an actual `file` leaf — found wherever it sits (a bare return, the MCP bridge's
{ text, files } record, or nested structure)."
agent collect_result_files(node: unknown) -> array[file] with pure {
  match (node) {
    case file(found) -> [found]
    case record(entries) -> {
      array.flatten(target = for (let value in record.values(target = entries)) {
        next collect_result_files(node = value)
      })
    }
    case array(items) -> {
      array.flatten(target = for (let item in items) {
        next collect_result_files(node = item)
      })
    }
    case _ -> []
  }
}

// ── served sessions: the serve skeleton, with token accounting and compaction ─
//
// Every chat bot hand-rolled the same skeleton: declare a request, keep the conversation in a
// handler var, run the tool loop once per message, append the reply. `serve_session` is that
// skeleton as a scoped provider — `use serve_session(...)` installs the handler, the continuation
// bridges its transport into `session_message` and (typically) never returns, a watch loop — plus
// the piece none of the hand-rolled ones had: the seam's `usage` metered into a context-occupancy
// figure, and a compaction pass when it outgrows a threshold. The policy is data (`compaction`),
// the one numeric comparison lives in `classify_budget`, and everything downstream dispatches on
// its sum. Transport specifics (a channel id, a thread ts) stay in the app's bridge agent: fold
// them into @content@ on the way in, route the returned reply on the way out.

@"How a session compacts its context once it outgrows the threshold. @threshold_tokens@ compares
against the freshest step's measured occupancy (`usage.input_tokens + output_tokens`); @prompt@
frames the summarization step; @keep_recent@ is how many trailing history elements survive
verbatim — extended forward to a clean `turn` boundary, so a call turn is never severed from the
results that answer it."
data compaction(threshold_tokens: integer, prompt: string, keep_recent: integer)

@"The default compaction policy both session shapes fall back to: compact past 100k tokens,
summarize under the standard self-note prompt, keep the last 8 history elements verbatim."
agent default_compaction() -> compaction {
  compaction(
    threshold_tokens = 100000,
    prompt = "Summarize the following conversation for your own future reference: keep every fact, decision, open question, and personal detail you might need again; drop the pleasantries and the dead ends. Answer with the summary only.",
    keep_recent = 8,
  )
}

@"The verdict on a measured context occupancy: still under the threshold, with the room left."
data within_budget(@"Tokens still free under the threshold." remaining: integer)

@"The verdict on a measured context occupancy: over the threshold — time to compact."
data over_budget(@"The measured occupancy that tripped the threshold." used: integer)

// The budget verdict. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type budget = within_budget | over_budget

@"Classify a measured context occupancy against the policy threshold — the compaction machinery's
one numeric comparison; everything downstream dispatches on the returned sum."
agent classify_budget(used: integer, policy: compaction) -> budget {
  if (used < policy.threshold_tokens) {
    within_budget(remaining = policy.threshold_tokens - used)
  } else {
    over_budget(used = used)
  }
}

@"The integer at a provider's usage field, or 0 when the field is absent — a provider that omits a
count still yields a usable measurement. Providers read their wire usage through this."
agent token_count(node: unknown) -> integer {
  match (node) {
    case integer(value) -> value
    case _ -> 0
  }
}

@"The first index at or after @start@ whose element is a plain `turn` — the clean cut for `compact`:
a `call_turn` must never be severed from the `result_turn` that answers it (a provider replays them
as a matched pair), so the kept tail always starts on a turn. No turn after @start@ means nothing
safely survives, and the whole history summarizes."
agent turn_boundary(history: array[types.message], start: integer) -> integer {
  for (let entry in array.slice(target = history, start = start, end = array.length(target = history)), var offset: integer = 0) {
    match (entry) {
      case types.turn(role => _, text => _, files => _) -> { break start + offset }
      case types.call_turn(calls => _) -> { next with { offset = offset + 1 } }
      case types.result_turn(results => _) -> { next with { offset = offset + 1 } }
    }
  } then (_entries) { array.length(target = history) }
}

@"A history element as summarizer-input text: turns as role-prefixed lines, tool traffic as compact
notes — enough for the summary to preserve what happened, nothing wire-shaped."
agent render_message(entry: types.message) -> string {
  match (entry) {
    case types.turn(role => role, text => text, files => files) -> {
      let notes = for (let attachment in files) { next f" [file ${json.stringify(value = attachment)}]" }
      let joined_notes = string.join(parts = notes, separator = "")
      f"${role}: ${text}${joined_notes}"
    }
    case types.call_turn(calls => calls) -> {
      let names = for (let call in calls) { next call.name }
      let joined_names = string.join(parts = names, separator = ", ")
      f"model: (called ${joined_names})"
    }
    case types.result_turn(results => results) -> {
      let texts = for (let result in results) { next f"${result.name} -> ${result.text}" }
      let joined_texts = string.join(parts = texts, separator = "\n")
      f"tools: ${joined_texts}"
    }
  }
}

@"Compress a conversation to fit its budget again: everything before the kept tail (the last
@policy.keep_recent@ elements, extended to a clean turn boundary) is summarized by one tool-less
model step under @policy.prompt@, and the summary replaces it as a single opening turn. A history
that is all tail comes back unchanged. Exported on its own so a hand-rolled loop can compact too —
`serve_session` is this plus the metering that decides when."
agent compact(history: array[types.message], policy: compaction) -> array[types.message] {
  let count = array.length(target = history)
  let wanted = if (count < policy.keep_recent) { 0 } else { count - policy.keep_recent }
  let cut = turn_boundary(history = history, start = wanted)
  if (cut == 0) {
    history
  } else {
    let head = array.slice(target = history, start = 0, end = cut)
    let tail = array.slice(target = history, start = cut, end = count)
    let rendered = for (let entry in head) { next render_message(entry = entry) }
    let joined = string.join(parts = rendered, separator = "\n")
    // The summarization step is a MODEL call, so it can fail like any other. A failed compaction must
    // neither lose the conversation nor crash a long-lived loop: catch the provider failure `reply`
    // re-raises and skip compaction this time (return the history unchanged); the next over-budget turn
    // tries again. This is the one place `compact` chooses to absorb `step_error` rather than propagate it.
    use handler {
      request prelude.throw(error: step_error) -> never { break history }
    }
    let summary = reply(history = [types.turn(
      role = "user",
      text = f"${policy.prompt}\n\n${joined}",
      files = [],
    )])
    array.concat(
      left = [types.turn(role = "user", text = f"[Summary of the earlier conversation]\n${summary}", files = [])],
      right = tail,
    )
  }
}

@"Raised once per incoming message of a served session; `serve_session`'s handler answers it with
the model's reply. The app's transport bridge performs it — fold any routing detail (a channel id, a
thread ts) into @content@ on the way in, route the returned reply on the way out."
request session_message(@"The incoming message, routing details folded in." content: string, @"The message's attachments." files: array[file]) -> string

@"A served session's durable state: the running conversation and the freshest measured context
occupancy — 0 until a step has run, and again right after a compaction until the next step
re-measures."
data session_state(conversation: array[types.message], used_tokens: integer)

@"One served turn's outcome: the reply, the advanced conversation, and the freshest measurement."
data session_turn(reply: string, conversation: array[types.message], used_tokens: integer)

@"A turn the provider FAILED mid-way: the conversation as far as it got (completed call / result
exchanges included) and the step error. `take_turn` hands failure back as a VALUE so a long-lived
caller — an app-owned event loop — can ABSORB it as one match arm (annotate the conversation, notify
a channel, await the next event) instead of unwinding; a one-shot caller re-raises it
(`infer_with_tools`, `serve_session`)."
data failed_turn(conversation: array[types.message], error: step_error)

type turn_result = session_turn | failed_turn

@"ONE tool-less step over @history@, folded to a `turn_result`: the shared closing move of the
budget exhaustion and the empty-final renudge — both must commit to an answer without tools, and
both hand a provider failure back as a value."
agent closing_step(history: array[types.message]) -> turn_result {
  match (infer_step(history = history, tool_metas = [])) {
    case inference_failed(error => error) -> failed_turn(conversation = history, error = error)
    case inferred(result => result) -> {
      let answer = match (result.step) {
        case types.step_final(text => text) -> text
        case types.step_call(calls => _) -> "(unexpected tool call without tools)"
      }
      session_turn(
        reply = answer,
        conversation = array.append(target = history, value = types.turn(role = "model", text = answer, files = [])),
        used_tokens = result.usage.input_tokens + result.usage.output_tokens,
      )
    }
  }
}

@"THE turn loop — advance @conversation@ by one model turn and hand back a VALUE either way: up to
@max_steps@ steps against the ambient provider, tool batches dispatched in parallel, the model's
reply appended as a model turn, and the LAST step's `input + output` carried out as the context
measurement (each step's input covers the whole context, so only the freshest figure is the
occupancy — steps do not add). Budget spent, one forced tool-less close commits to an answer; an
EMPTY final is renudged once for a plain-text answer (an empty here is a lost answer, not a signal —
a caller whose protocol gives the empty reply meaning still receives the second empty as-is). A
provider failure at any step returns `failed_turn` rather than unwinding, so an app-owned event loop
absorbs it as one match arm. This is the ai package's ONE loop: sessions (`serve_session`) and
resident agents (an app-owned `observation` handler over a `region` nursery) are compositions over
it. The knobs are policy the caller owns: @tool_budget_milliseconds@ deadlines every tool call
(null = no deadline), @unbounded_tool_names@ exempts the named human-latency tools from that
deadline, and @nudge_empty_reply@ = false accepts an empty final as-is — for a caller
whose protocol gives the empty reply meaning (a watcher that stays quiet), so a quiet turn does not
pay the renudge's extra step."
agent take_turn[effect E lacks time.race_settled](
  conversation: array[types.message],
  tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  max_steps: integer,
  @"The per-tool-call budget in milliseconds — every call races it via `time.with_deadline`, and a timeout
comes back to the model as an error result. `null` (the default) sets no deadline." tool_budget_milliseconds: number | null ?= null,
  @"The metadata names of tools EXEMPT from the deadline race — the human-latency tools: an
approval-gated adapter that blocks on an operator's click, an MCP call that may park on an OAuth
authorization. The doctrine: a deadline bounds MACHINE latency, and a pending ask or park is visible
in the admin surface while it waits, so such a call may be left unbounded rather than cancelled
mid-ask. Defaults to none — every tool stays under the budget." unbounded_tool_names: array[string] ?= [],
  @"Whether an empty final reply is renudged once for a plain-text answer (the interactive default).
Pass `false` when the empty reply is a meaningful signal in your protocol." nudge_empty_reply: boolean ?= true,
) -> turn_result {
  let tool_metas = ensure_unique_names(metas = for (let tool in tools) { next reflection.get_metadata(value = tool) })
  let outcome = for (let _step in array.range(start = 0, end = max_steps), var state: session_turn = session_turn(reply = "", conversation = conversation, used_tokens = 0)) {
    match (infer_step(history = state.conversation, tool_metas = tool_metas)) {
      case inference_failed(error => error) -> { break failed_turn(conversation = state.conversation, error = error) }
      case inferred(result => result) -> {
        let measured = result.usage.input_tokens + result.usage.output_tokens
        match (result.step) {
          case types.step_final(text => reply_text) -> {
            break session_turn(
              reply = reply_text,
              conversation = array.append(target = state.conversation, value = types.turn(role = "model", text = reply_text, files = [])),
              used_tokens = measured,
            )
          }
          case types.step_call(calls => calls) -> {
            let results = parallel for (let call in calls) {
              next dispatch_one[E](tools = tools, call = call, budget_milliseconds = tool_budget_milliseconds, unbounded_tool_names = unbounded_tool_names)
            }
            let with_calls = array.append(target = state.conversation, value = types.call_turn(calls = calls))
            next with { state = session_turn(reply = "", conversation = array.append(target = with_calls, value = types.result_turn(results = results)), used_tokens = measured) }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  } then (_steps) {
    // Budget exhausted: the shared forced tool-less close.
    closing_step(history = array.append(target = state.conversation, value = budget_exhausted_turn()))
  }
  // An EMPTY final is a lost answer IN AN INTERACTIVE EXCHANGE: renudge ONCE with an explicit ask,
  // and accept whatever the extra step says — a second empty comes back as-is (honestly) rather than
  // looping. A caller whose protocol gives the empty reply meaning (a quiet watcher) opts out with
  // @nudge_empty_reply@ = false and pays nothing. A failed turn passes through untouched.
  match (outcome) {
    case session_turn(reply => reply_text, conversation => advanced, used_tokens => used) -> {
      if (reply_text == "" && nudge_empty_reply) {
        closing_step(history = array.append(target = advanced, value = empty_reply_nudge()))
      } else {
        session_turn(reply = reply_text, conversation = advanced, used_tokens = used)
      }
    }
    case failed -> failed
  }
}

@"Serve a chat session for the extent of @continuation@ — the hand-rolled serve skeleton as one
provider, with token metering and automatic compaction. The handler's var carries the conversation
across messages (durable by construction: it survives a runtime restart like any run state); each
`session_message` runs one tool loop and answers with the reply. After each turn the freshest
measured occupancy is classified against @threshold_tokens@: over it, the conversation compacts
(`compact`) under @compact_prompt@, keeping the last @keep_recent@ elements verbatim. The ambient
provider (`use gemini.provider(...)` …) and any @E@ handlers are installed by the caller, exactly as
for `infer_with_tools`; the continuation bridges its transport into `session_message` and typically
never returns (a `watch_messages` loop)."
agent serve_session[R, effect E lacks time.race_settled](
  @"The conversation the model starts from (PRIOR TURNS only — standing instructions belong in the
provider's `system` parameter, which every provider takes and compaction never touches; anything put
here is history, and the first compaction folds it into the summary)." context: array[types.message],
  @"The tools of every turn's loop." tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  @"The per-message step budget." max_steps: integer,
  @"The compaction policy — one `compaction(...)` datum; `null` (the default) is `default_compaction`." policy: compaction | null ?= null,
  @"The per-tool-call budget in milliseconds, or `null` (the default) for no deadline — see `take_turn`." tool_budget_milliseconds: number | null ?= null,
  @"Tool names exempt from the deadline race (human-latency tools) — see `take_turn`." unbounded_tool_names: array[string] ?= [],
  @"The session body: bridges a transport into `session_message`." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, session_message},
) -> R {
  let effective_policy = match (policy) {
    case null -> default_compaction()
    case given -> given
  }
  use handler (var state: session_state = session_state(conversation = context, used_tokens = 0)) {
    request session_message(content: string, files: array[file]) {
      let asked = array.append(target = state.conversation, value = types.turn(role = "user", text = content, files = files))
      match (take_turn[E](conversation = asked, tools = tools, max_steps = max_steps, tool_budget_milliseconds = tool_budget_milliseconds, unbounded_tool_names = unbounded_tool_names)) {
        // A served session is one-shot per message, so a provider failure re-raises at the perform
        // site for the app's transport bridge (the `session_message` caller) to handle.
        case failed_turn(conversation => _, error => error) -> { prelude.throw(error = error) }
        case session_turn(reply => reply_text, conversation => advanced, used_tokens => used) -> {
          match (classify_budget(used = used, policy = effective_policy)) {
            case within_budget(remaining => _) -> {
              next reply_text with { state = session_state(conversation = advanced, used_tokens = used) }
            }
            case over_budget(used => _) -> {
              // The compacted context's occupancy is unknown until the next step measures it; 0 says
              // "not yet measured", never an estimate.
              next reply_text with { state = session_state(conversation = compact(history = advanced, policy = effective_policy), used_tokens = 0) }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

// ── event vocabulary: observations and scheduled prompts ─────────────────────
//
// The resident-agent pattern: sources and watches fork into a `region` nursery and push
// `observation`s; a stateful handler injects each report and advances the conversation with
// `take_turn`. That serving handler is `serve_observations` below — the resident sibling of
// `serve_session`, and like it a composition OVER the package's one loop. The REGION wiring stays
// with the app (the nursery, its ceiling, the forks, and the `region.crashed` interpretation):
// lifetime is the app's data, not this package's. Watches stay ordinary tools the app writes: a
// hand-authored adapter (with its own model-facing description) that approves and forks a fiber
// however the app sees fit.

@"THE event→model channel: a background fiber (an always-on source such as a chat watcher, or a
started watch) pushes @content@ (plus any @files@) from @source@ into the model's context.
FIRE-AND-FORGET (`-> null`): a fiber only reports — the app's serving handler injects the report as
a user turn, advances the conversation (`take_turn`), and routes the reply wherever it belongs.
@files@ ride the injection turn's `files` slot — the same text + files split as `types.turn` — so
the provider's existing inline policy shows the model any attached image. @author@ rides the
injection turn's `author`, so the triggered turn's requests carry the right abuse-attribution
metadata (`ai.attribution`)."
request observation(@"Which fiber is reporting (e.g. \"discord:<channel>\"); it labels the report's injection turn." source: string, @"The report to inject into the model's context." content: string, @"Any files attached to the report (a Discord / Slack attachment), injected on the report's turn so the provider inlines them for the model; defaults to none." files: array[file] ?= [], @"The opaque end-user tag of the human behind this report (e.g. a hashed chat-user id), or `null` for a system report (a scheduled tick)." author: string | null ?= null) -> null

@"A watch body reduced to its essence: on every occurrence of @schedule@, inject the freshly
computed @prompt@ into the model's context as an observation from @source@ — the scheduled
self-prompt every periodic watcher (check a repo, track a topic, look at a calendar) hand-rolled as
its own `time.watch` adapter. @prompt@ is an agent so each tick recomputes it (an env-configured
target re-reads, a date renders current); its effects @E@ flow to the app's handlers. Never returns:
fork it into the app's nursery and cancel its fiber to stop."
agent watch_prompt[effect E](
  @"Which watcher is reporting; labels the injection turn (e.g. \"repo-issues\")." source: string,
  @"Computes the report to inject, fresh at every occurrence." prompt: agent (value: null) -> string with E,
  @"When occurrences fire: `time.interval(...)` or `time.cron(...)`." schedule: time.schedule,
) -> never with {...E, observation} | io {
  agent tick(time: number) -> null with {...E, observation} {
    observation(source = source, content = prompt(value = null))
  }
  time.watch(schedule = schedule, deliver_to = tick)
}

@"Serve a RESIDENT agent's observations for the extent of @continuation@ — the resident sibling of
`serve_session`, and the one home of the absorb / deliver / compact skeleton every resident app
hand-rolled: each `observation` injects as a user turn (source-labelled, files and author riding it),
one `take_turn` advances the conversation, and the reply leaves through @deliver_to@. THE RESIDENT
PROTOCOL IS BUILT IN: an empty (blank) reply means 'nothing to say' — it is not delivered and not
renudged, so a quiet watcher tick costs exactly one model step. A provider failure is ABSORBED as one
match arm — @deliver_to@ gets a one-line notice, the conversation is annotated, and the loop awaits
the next event — because a resident must outlive a failed step. After each turn the measured
occupancy classifies against @policy@ and an over-budget conversation compacts. The conversation
starts EMPTY: standing instructions belong in the provider's `system` parameter, which compaction
never touches. Install this INSIDE your providers and ABOVE your `region.watch` call; the region
wiring — the nursery, its ceiling, the registry, the forks — stays yours."
agent serve_observations[R, effect E lacks time.race_settled](
  @"The tools of every turn's loop." tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  @"The per-observation step budget." max_steps: integer,
  @"Where a turn's non-empty reply (and a step-failure notice) is delivered." deliver_to: agent (reply: string) -> null with E,
  @"The compaction policy — one `compaction(...)` datum; `null` (the default) is `default_compaction`." policy: compaction | null ?= null,
  @"The per-tool-call budget in milliseconds, or `null` (the default) for no deadline — see `take_turn`." tool_budget_milliseconds: number | null ?= null,
  @"Tool names exempt from the deadline race (human-latency tools) — see `take_turn`." unbounded_tool_names: array[string] ?= [],
  @"The resident body: forks its sources and typically never returns (`region.watch`)." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, observation},
) -> R {
  let effective_policy = match (policy) {
    case null -> default_compaction()
    case given -> given
  }
  use handler (var state: session_state = session_state(conversation = [], used_tokens = 0)) {
    request observation(source: string, content: string, files: array[file], author: string | null) {
      let injected = array.append(target = state.conversation, value = types.turn(role = "user", text = f"Notification from ${source}:\n${content}", files = files, author = author))
      match (take_turn[E](conversation = injected, tools = tools, max_steps = max_steps, tool_budget_milliseconds = tool_budget_milliseconds, unbounded_tool_names = unbounded_tool_names, nudge_empty_reply = false)) {
        // ABSORB a provider failure: notify, annotate, await the next event — the resident loop
        // must survive a failed step, not unwind on it.
        case failed_turn(conversation => at_failure, error => error) -> {
          let rendered = json.stringify(value = error)
          deliver_to(reply = f"(model error — the turn was dropped: ${rendered})")
          let marked = array.append(target = at_failure, value = types.turn(role = "user", text = f"[model step failed: ${rendered}. The turn was abandoned; context is preserved.]", files = []))
          next null with { state = session_state(conversation = marked, used_tokens = state.used_tokens) }
        }
        case session_turn(reply => reply_text, conversation => advanced, used_tokens => used) -> {
          // A blank reply is the protocol's silence — deliver nothing.
          if (string.is_blank(value = reply_text)) {
            null
          } else {
            deliver_to(reply = reply_text)
          }
          match (classify_budget(used = used, policy = effective_policy)) {
            case within_budget(remaining => _) -> {
              next null with { state = session_state(conversation = advanced, used_tokens = used) }
            }
            case over_budget(used => _) -> {
              next null with { state = session_state(conversation = compact(history = advanced, policy = effective_policy), used_tokens = 0) }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

// ── the supervisor: a restart loop with backoff and same-reason suppression ────
//
// A resident that must OUTLIVE its own crashes needs a supervisor: run one session, and when it ends
// with a recoverable reason, wait a backoff and start a fresh one. The mechanism is generic — the
// session body, the restart-notice sink, and the backoff numbers are all DATA — so an app keeps every
// specific it owns: WHICH crashes are fatal (its session re-raises those PAST `supervise`, which only
// ever sees a RETURNED recoverable reason), WHAT a restart notice says, and WHERE it goes. This is the
// lifetime sibling of `serve_observations`: that one keeps a resident alive across a failed STEP; this
// one keeps it alive across a failed SESSION.

@"Supervise a restartable @session@ FOREVER: run one attempt, and when it ends by RETURNING a
recoverable crash reason, call @on_restart@, wait the current backoff, and start a fresh attempt. The
backoff starts at @initial_delay_ms@ and grows by @factor@ each time the SAME reason repeats (a
sustained outage), capped at @max_delay_ms@; a CHANGED reason resets it to the floor. @on_restart@'s
@repeated@ is whether this reason equals the immediately preceding one, so the sink can shorten (or
suppress) a repeat notice to one quiet line. Fatal-vs-restartable is the SESSION's decision, not this
loop's: a session that hits an unrecoverable error re-raises it, and it propagates through `supervise`
UNTOUCHED to a handler the app installed above; `supervise` only ever sees the reason a session hands
back by RETURNING, and every such return is a restart. Returns `never` — the loop ends only when a
session's fatal re-raise unwinds past it."
agent supervise[effect E](
  @"One attempt: run the resident until it ends recoverably and RETURN the crash reason to restart from. A fatal error re-raises past `supervise` instead of returning." session: agent (value: null) -> string with E,
  @"The restart-notice sink, called once per restart with the returned @reason@ and whether it @repeated@ the immediately preceding reason (so a repeat can be shortened or dropped)." on_restart: agent (reason: string, repeated: boolean) -> null with E,
  @"The backoff floor in milliseconds — the first wait, and the wait a changed reason resets to. Default 30s." initial_delay_ms: number ?= 30000.0,
  @"The factor the backoff grows by while the same reason repeats. Default 2." factor: number ?= 2.0,
  @"The backoff ceiling in milliseconds — the growing delay never exceeds it. Default 15 minutes." max_delay_ms: number ?= 900000.0,
) -> never with E | io {
  forever (var delay: number = initial_delay_ms, var last_reason: string = "") {
    let reason = session(value = null)
    let repeated = reason == last_reason
    on_restart(reason = reason, repeated = repeated)
    time.sleep(milliseconds = delay)
    // Grow the delay while the reason repeats (a sustained outage); reset to the floor when it changes.
    let next_delay = if (repeated) { math.min(left = delay * factor, right = max_delay_ms) } else { initial_delay_ms }
    next with { delay = next_delay, last_reason = reason }
  }
}

// ── ambient-context middleware ────────────────────────────────────────────────

@"Middleware over the seam: EVERY `infer_step`, prepend @inject@'s freshly computed text as a user
turn and re-perform outward to the ambient provider — the generalization of the bot-side pattern
that re-injects the current time and a memory index each turn. Install it INSIDE the provider
(`use gemini.provider(...)` then `use ai.with_context(inject = ...)`), so the re-performed step
reaches the model. An EMPTY injection skips the extra turn; the injected turn is never part of the
conversation a loop persists (it is derived fresh each step, so persisting it would double it).
`infer_object` passes through un-injected — structured extraction states its own context."
agent with_context[R, effect E](
  @"Computes the note to prepend this step — ambient state (the time, a memory index), fresh each step." inject: agent (value: null) -> string with {...E, infer_step},
  @"The program to run with the injection installed." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, infer_step},
) -> R with E | infer_step {
  use handler {
    request infer_step(history: array[types.message], tool_metas: array[reflection.agent_metadata]) -> step_outcome {
      let note = inject(value = null)
      let injected = if (note == "") {
        history
      } else {
        array.concat(left = [types.turn(role = "user", text = note, files = [])], right = history)
      }
      // Re-performing escalates PAST this handler to the ambient provider — the outward re-perform
      // semantics a rethrown request uses.
      next infer_step(history = injected, tool_metas = tool_metas)
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

// ── wire tool names ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Katari enforces namespaces, so a tool's derived name is ALWAYS qualified (`katari_verify.count_letters`).
// A provider's name vocabulary is narrower than that: Anthropic and OpenAI validate every tool name —
// on the request's tool declarations AND on the tool_use / tool_call blocks replayed in history —
// against `^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$`, which forbids the dot (a qualified name 400s). So a provider whose
// vocabulary is too narrow shapes the qualified name down to a wire name here, and reverses the model's
// pick back to the real name before the loop dispatches it (the loop resolves a call by matching
// `tool_call.name` against tool metadata, so that name MUST stay the real qualified one). Gemini's
// vocabulary already admits the dot, so its provider never consults this — a provider only maps when
// its wire demands it.
//
// The map is a pure function of the step's `tool_metas` (their order included), so every step rebuilds
// the identical map from the same tools and no durable state is needed to keep request and response
// agreeing on a name.

@"A deterministic two-way mapping between a tool's real (qualified) name and the shortened name a
provider sends on the wire. @real_to_wire@ is keyed by the qualified name (used when encoding a tool
declaration or replaying a call turn), @wire_to_real@ by the wire name (used to reverse the model's
pick); both are total over one step's tools — see @build_name_map@."
data name_map(
  @"Qualified tool name → wire name." real_to_wire: record[string],
  @"Wire name → qualified tool name." wire_to_real: record[string],
)

@"Fold a wire name onto a qualified name: every character outside a provider's `[a-zA-Z0-9_-]` name
alphabet (the dot of a qualified name, above all) folds to `_`, then the result is capped at the
providers' 64-character limit."
agent sanitize_name(@"The qualified tool name." name: string) -> string {
  let allowed = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789_-"
  let mapped = for (let character in string.split(value = name, separator = "")) {
    if (string.contains(value = allowed, search = character)) {
      next character
    } else {
      next "_"
    }
  }
  string.slice(value = string.join(parts = mapped, separator = ""), start = 0, end = 64)
}

@"Disambiguate a colliding wire name with an ordinal suffix (`_2`, `_3`, …), reserving room so the
result still fits the 64-character cap."
agent suffixed_name(@"The colliding base wire name." base: string, @"The 1-based ordinal." index: integer) -> string {
  let suffix = f"_${string.to_string(value = index)}"
  let room = 64 - string.length(value = suffix)
  f"${string.slice(value = base, start = 0, end = room)}${suffix}"
}

@"Choose a free wire name for @real@ given the wire names already @taken@: its sanitized form, or —
when sanitizing collapsed two distinct qualified names onto one token, or truncation shared a prefix —
the first free ordinal-suffixed form. The suffix is decided by @taken@'s size (processing order), so
the choice is deterministic."
agent assign_wire_name(@"The wire names already assigned this step." taken: record[string], @"The qualified name to place." real: string) -> string {
  let base = sanitize_name(name = real)
  let count = record.size(target = taken)
  // Indices 1..count+1 yield count+1 DISTINCT candidates (base, then `base_2`, `base_3`, …); at most
  // `count` names are taken, so at least one candidate is free and the loop always breaks. `chosen`
  // stays `base` only in the unreachable no-break case.
  for (let index in array.range(start = 1, end = count + 2), var chosen: string = base) {
    let candidate = if (index == 1) { base } else { suffixed_name(base = base, index = index) }
    if (record.has(target = taken, key = candidate)) {
      next with { chosen = chosen }
    } else {
      break candidate
    }
  } then (_indices) { chosen }
}

@"Build the deterministic wire-name @name_map@ for one step's tools. Walking @tool_metas@ in order,
each tool's qualified name is assigned a free wire name against the names already placed, so the same
tool list always yields the same map — which is why the map needs no durable state (see the section
note). A provider whose wire vocabulary admits the qualified name (Gemini) never calls this."
agent build_name_map(@"The step's tools, in order." tool_metas: array[reflection.agent_metadata]) -> name_map {
  for (let meta in tool_metas, var acc: name_map = name_map(real_to_wire = record.empty(), wire_to_real = record.empty())) {
    let wire = assign_wire_name(taken = acc.wire_to_real, real = meta.name)
    next with {
      acc = name_map(
        real_to_wire = record.set(target = acc.real_to_wire, key = meta.name, value = wire),
        wire_to_real = record.set(target = acc.wire_to_real, key = wire, value = meta.name),
      )
    }
  } then (_metas) { acc }
}

@"The wire name for a qualified tool name — used to encode a tool declaration and to re-encode a
replayed call turn. Falls back to the qualified name for a tool absent from the map (never happens for
a map built from the same step's tools)."
agent wire_name_of(@"The step's name map." map: name_map, @"The qualified tool name." real: string) -> string {
  match (record.get(target = map.real_to_wire, key = real)) {
    case null -> real
    case wire -> wire
  }
}

@"The qualified tool name for a wire name — used to reverse the model's pick so the loop dispatches by
the real name. Falls back to the wire name itself for an unknown name (a hallucinated tool), which the
loop then reports as an unknown tool."
agent real_name_of(@"The step's name map." map: name_map, @"The wire name the model returned." wire: string) -> string {
  match (record.get(target = map.wire_to_real, key = wire)) {
    case null -> wire
    case real -> real
  }
}
